Submodel V1 (constraints (1)-(12n): stability + centre of mass), n = 18-22.
150 runs: 5 sizes x 10 instances x 3 solvers. Objective (1), lower is better.
All returned packings verified feasible, including the CoM pyramid (0 violations).

MILP (Gurobi) and BS (beam search) run under a 3s time limit.
WFBF is NOT time limited: it is a single deterministic pass, and its runtime is measured, not budgeted.
WFBF below runs in "corner" mode (candidates restricted to the origin and corner points opened by
placed items), not the paper's literal Algorithm 1 (every beta-grid position). On these instances
beta=1, so literal enumeration is ~1.7M positions/item/rotation and takes several seconds per
instance instead of sub-millisecond - see Results_WFBF_grid_vs_corner_n18-22 for the direct
comparison (both modes, same instances). corner mode is faster AND gives a BETTER objective here,
so the WFBF numbers below are not disadvantaged by the approximation; only the WFBF TIME column
should not be read as comparable to the paper's own WFBF timings.
All three see the same derived weights and CoMs (seed=1, alpha=0.8), so the objectives are comparable.
MILP is 0/10 optimal at every size: it reaches TIME_LIMIT with ~100% gap, i.e. the bound has not moved
off zero in 3s, so the gap column measures the missing bound, not the quality of the found solution.

